%% rv_agent.glp - Rendezvous Agent
%%
%% A minimal GLP agent that coordinates reconnection between agents
%% that lost connectivity due to IP address changes, and the redemption of
%% an invitation link (GNA Section 6, "The rendezvous agent").
%%
%% - Accepts cold-calls from any agent (no friends list, no social graph)
%% - Verifies each party's reconnection attestation with the language's
%%   signature/2
%% - Learns each party's current public address via peer_address (reading
%%   the address at which the networking layer observes it), and refuses
%%   where the layer observes none
%% - Relays to each the address it observes for the other, to coordinate
%%   hole-punching
%%
%% Protocol:
%%   1. A detects IP change, cold-calls:
%%      reconnect(A_pk, B_pk, SigB, ReplyA)
%%   2. B detects A went silent, cold-calls:
%%      available(B_pk, A_pk, SigA, ReplyB)
%%   3. The RV agent reads each one's address from the networking layer via
%%      peer_address(Pk?, PeerAddress) --- address(Addr), or none, on which
%%      it refuses --- matches the two requests, and tells each to punch to
%%      the other's address via its Reply: A gets punch(AddrB), B punch(AddrA)
%%
%% Admission by key (GSA, Section "The Secure Grassroots Social Graph",
%% "Distribution and Activation", paragraph "Reconnection": "the rendezvous
%% agent checks that each request is signed by the key it names and that the
%% two signatures are the two friends' over their pair ... The rendezvous
%% agent checks keys, so an update of the Grassroots App leaves the
%% signatures friends hold good"; GSA #2's task of 2026-11-07: "No version
%% numbers and no source identities"; GNA Section 6, "Reconnection"):
%%   on befriending, each friend signs the one pair attest(PkA, PkB), PkA
%%   being the lexicographically smaller key --- PkA @< PkB, the standard
%%   order of constants (GLP-Spec appendix-guards) --- and gives the
%%   signature to the other.  A reconnection request from A for B carries
%%   B's signature over the pair; B's availability for A carries A's,
%%   over the same pair; neither names a separate signer.  The agent takes
%%   each signature apart with signature/2, which assigns
%%   signed(Key, Hash, Term) --- the signer's key, the source identity of
%%   the signing program and the term signed --- where it verifies, and
%%   unsigned where it does not; admit_reconnect/6 and admit_available/6
%%   dispatch on that:
%%
%%       reconnect from A for B:   signed(B, _, attest(PkA, PkB))   admits
%%       available from B for A:   signed(A, _, attest(PkA, PkB))   admits
%%
%%   the signer being the other party the request names and {PkA, PkB} =
%%   {A, B} with PkA @< PkB.  The source identity Hash is not read: the agent
%%   checks keys and no program identity, so a signature made under an
%%   earlier version of the Grassroots App stays good, and the two of a pair
%%   may have been made under different ones.  Anything else --- unsigned,
%%   another signer, another term, or the pair out of that order --- is
%%   refused with rejected.
%%
%%   Under the plain REPL the tests present no verified signature but their
%%   own: through the agent they exercise the reject path, but for tests 22
%%   and 25, whose test program signs the pair under its own key: its
%%   available is admitted and reaches peer_address/2, which with no
%%   networking layer bound assigns none (IGLP, "One interface"), and is
%%   refused there.  The dispatch on what signature/2 reports, and on what
%%   peer_address/2 reports, is given their answers directly.
%%
%% Invitation links (GNA Section 6, "Invitation links"):
%%   a link is the term invitation(I, Rv, RvAddr, Serial, Expiry), signed by
%%   the inviter I with sign/3: I's key, the rendezvous server's key and
%%   address, a serial I keeps (an Integer) and the expiry, an hour after
%%   creation, in Unix milliseconds, now/1's unit.
%%   1. I, over its session with this server, registers the serial as
%%      unused:  register(I_pk, Link, ReplyI)
%%      admitted where signature/2 reports Link signed by I and naming I as
%%      the inviter, and I has not registered that serial before; else
%%      ReplyI = rejected.  An admitted registration waits as
%%      invited(I_pk, Serial, ReplyI) on the pending list.
%%   2. The recipient R redeems the link:  redeem(R_pk, Signed, ReplyR),
%%      Signed the term redeem(R_pk, Link) signed by R with sign/3, a signed
%%      term being ground (GNA 0147658), admitted where signature/2 reports
%%      Signed signed by R over redeem(R_pk, Link), and Link signed by the
%%      inviter it names, the link names this agent, its Rv being the agent's own key,
%%      Id of rv_server/2, and now/1 is before its expiry; the agent then
%%      reads the address at which the layer observes each of I and R by
%%      peer_address/2, refusing where it observes none, and accepts only
%%      where the serial is registered for I and unused, which it marks used,
%%      used(I_pk, Serial, Expiry), so that a link admits one first contact.
%%   3. The relay: ReplyI = punch(AddrR), ReplyR = punch(AddrI).  Each calls
%%      punch_udp/1 towards the other, and the deterministic initiator, the
%%      endpoint with the smaller key, connects (GNA, "IP connection").
%%   A refused redemption changes nothing: the serial stays unused and I
%%   waits.  An unredeemed registration waits until its serial is redeemed;
%%   a used serial is kept until its link's expiry and then dropped: the
%%   agent reads now/1 at every request and, before it acts on the request,
%%   drops each used(I_pk, Serial, Expiry) with now at or past Expiry, the
%%   link's expiry as the redemption that used it carried.  Once dropped, the
%%   agent holds no record of the serial, so a later registration of it is
%%   admitted as a first one; a redemption of the dropped link is refused,
%%   the link having expired.
%%
%% System predicates:
%%   now(T) — root self.glp (GLP-Spec catalogue): the current Unix
%%     timestamp in milliseconds.
%%   signature(Signed?, Signature) — root self.glp (GLP-Spec catalogue):
%%     signed(Key, Hash, Term) or unsigned.
%%   peer_address(Pk?, PeerAddress) — root self.glp (GLP-Spec catalogue):
%%     address(S), S the address (ip:port, a String) at which the networking
%%     layer observes peer Pk, or none where it observes no address for Pk.
%%   K1 @< K2 — guard (GLP-Spec catalogue): K1 precedes K2 in the standard
%%     order of constants; on two keys, strings, by the codes of their
%%     characters, lexicographically.


%% =============================================================================
%% TYPE DEFINITIONS
%% =============================================================================

%% Reply sent back to agents via their reply variable: the address to punch
%% to, or rejected
SignalReply ::= punch(String) ; rejected.

%% Messages arriving via cold-call on the agent's input stream
SignalMsg ::= reconnect(Key, Key, SignedTerm, SignalReply?)
              %% reconnect(A_pk, B_pk, SigB, ReplyA) — SigB is B's
              %% signature over the pair attest(PkA, PkB), PkA @< PkB
            ; available(Key, Key, SignedTerm, SignalReply?)
              %% available(B_pk, A_pk, SigA, ReplyB) — SigA is A's
              %% signature over the same pair
            ; register(Key, SignedTerm, SignalReply?)
              %% register(I_pk, Link, ReplyI) — the inviter I registers the
              %% serial of its invitation link Link as unused; ReplyI is
              %% the address of the recipient who redeems it, punch(AddrR),
              %% or rejected
            ; redeem(Key, SignedTerm, SignalReply?).
              %% redeem(R_pk, Signed, ReplyR) — the recipient R redeems a
              %% link: Signed is redeem(R_pk, Link) signed by R; ReplyR is
              %% punch(AddrI), or rejected

%% Pending entries awaiting a match
PendingEntry ::= needs(Key, Key, String, SignalReply?)
                 %% needs(A_pk, B_pk, AddrA, ReplyA?) — A wants to reconnect to B
               ; ready(Key, Key, String, SignalReply?)
                 %% ready(B_pk, A_pk, AddrB, ReplyB?) — B is available for A
               ; invited(Key, Integer, SignalReply?)
                 %% invited(I_pk, Serial, ReplyI?) — I's serial, unused
               ; used(Key, Integer, Integer).
                 %% used(I_pk, Serial, Expiry) — I's serial, redeemed, kept
                 %% until its link's expiry
PendingList ::= [] ; [PendingEntry | PendingList].

%% A message as it arrives on the agent's network input: msg(Q, M), Q the
%% agent's own key, to which the sender addressed it, and M the message
RvNetMsg ::= msg(Key, SignalMsg).
RvNetIn ::= [] ; [RvNetMsg | RvNetIn].
SignalIn ::= [] ; [SignalMsg | SignalIn].

%% =============================================================================
%% THE SERVER'S ENTRY
%% =============================================================================

%% rv_server(Id, NetIn): the rendezvous server's agent, as a GLP runtime over
%% the headless layer boots it (networking/glue/bin/rendezvous_server.dart):
%% Id is the agent's own key and NetIn the network input, each element
%% msg(Id, M) with M a request sent to this agent.  The envelope is taken
%% off and the agent runs over what it carried, with nothing pending, knowing
%% its own key Id, by which it tells a link that names it.
exported procedure rv_server(Key?, RvNetIn?).
rv_server(Id, NetIn) :-
    rv_unwrap(NetIn?, In),
    rv_agent(Id?, In?, []).

%% The agent's initial goal, every agent's, agent(ch(_?,_),ch(_?,_)) (GLP-Spec
%% maglp.tex, Definition "Multiagent GLP"; IGLP, Implementation Notes, "Boot":
%% "a deployed agent is spawned by its application with the same initial
%% goal"): the agent is told nothing at boot and reads nothing from its
%% person, whose output it closes; self_key/1 gives it its own key, and
%% agent/2 starts the server's agent, rv_server/2, on the network input.
exported procedure agent(Channel(Stream(Constant), Stream(Constant))?, Channel(RvNetIn, Stream(NetMsg(Constant)))?).
agent(ch(_, []), ch(N, [])) :- self_key(K), rv_server(K?, N?).

procedure rv_unwrap(RvNetIn?, SignalIn).
rv_unwrap([msg(_, M) | Ms], [M? | In?]) :- rv_unwrap(Ms?, In).
rv_unwrap([], []).

%% =============================================================================
%% MAIN LOOP
%% =============================================================================

%% rv_agent(Id, In, Pending): the agent of key Id over its requests In, with
%% Pending the requests awaiting their match and the serials of the links it
%% has registered or matched.  At each request it reads the clock by now/1,
%% and rv_request/5 acts on the request at that time.  The guard grounds Id.
exported procedure rv_agent(Key?, Stream(SignalMsg)?, PendingList?).
rv_agent(Id, [M|In], Pending) :-
    ground(Id?) |
    now(T),
    rv_request(Id?, M?, T?, Pending?, Pending1),
    rv_agent(Id?, In?, Pending1?).
rv_agent(_, [], _).

%% rv_request(Id, M, T, Pending, Pending1): the request M at time T.  The used
%% serials whose links have expired by T are dropped first, by prune_used/3.
%% The guard grounds T.
procedure rv_request(Key?, SignalMsg?, Integer?, PendingList?, PendingList).

%% A requests reconnection to B, presenting SigB, B's signature over the
%% pair.  signature/2 takes it apart and admit_reconnect/6 dispatches on
%% what it reports.
rv_request(_, reconnect(A, B, Sig, ReplyA?), T, Pending, Pending1?) :-
    integer(T?) |
    prune_used(T?, Pending?, Pending0),
    signature(Sig?, S),
    admit_reconnect(A?, B?, S?, ReplyA, Pending0?, Pending1).

%% B is available for reconnection from A, presenting SigA, A's signature
%% over the same pair; verified as the reconnect request is, by
%% admit_available/6.
rv_request(_, available(B, A, Sig, ReplyB?), T, Pending, Pending1?) :-
    integer(T?) |
    prune_used(T?, Pending?, Pending0),
    signature(Sig?, S),
    admit_available(B?, A?, S?, ReplyB, Pending0?, Pending1).

%% The inviter I registers the serial of its invitation link, over its
%% session with this server; the link carries what is checked.  Whether the
%% link has expired, and whether it names this agent, is decided at its
%% redemption.
rv_request(_, register(I, Link, ReplyI?), T, Pending, Pending1?) :-
    integer(T?) |
    prune_used(T?, Pending?, Pending0),
    signature(Link?, S),
    admit_register(I?, S?, ReplyI, Pending0?, Pending1).

%% The recipient R redeems the link it was sent, presenting Signed, the
%% term redeem(R, Link) signed by R; admit_redeemer/7 dispatches on what
%% signature/2 reports for it, and admit_redeem/7 on what it reports for the
%% link, on the agent's own key Id and on the clock.
rv_request(Id, redeem(R, Signed, ReplyR?), T, Pending, Pending1?) :-
    integer(T?) |
    prune_used(T?, Pending?, Pending0),
    signature(Signed?, SR),
    admit_redeemer(Id?, R?, SR?, T?, ReplyR, Pending0?, Pending1).

%% =============================================================================
%% prune_used: drop the used serials whose links have expired
%% =============================================================================

%% prune_used(T, Pending, Pending1): Pending1 is Pending without the entries
%% used(I, Serial, Expiry) with T at or past Expiry; every other entry is
%% kept, in its order.
exported procedure prune_used(Integer?, PendingList?, PendingList).

%% A used serial whose link has expired: dropped.
prune_used(T, [used(_, _, E)|Ps], Ps1?) :-
    integer(T?), integer(E?), T? >= E? |
    prune_used(T?, Ps?, Ps1).

%% Any other entry, a used serial whose link has not expired among them:
%% kept.
prune_used(T, [P|Ps], [P?|Ps1?]) :-
    otherwise |
    prune_used(T?, Ps?, Ps1).

prune_used(_, [], []).

%% =============================================================================
%% admit_reconnect: dispatch on what signature/2 reports for A's request
%% =============================================================================

exported procedure admit_reconnect(Key?, Key?, Signature?, SignalReply,
                                   PendingList?, PendingList).

%% B signed the pair, A's key the smaller: admitted, under whatever program
%% B signed it.  Only then does the RV agent read A's address from the
%% networking layer via the root's peer_address (so A does not need to
%% self-report its address), and match_reconnect/6 dispatches on what it
%% reports.  The guard grounds A and B.
admit_reconnect(A, B, signed(K, _, attest(P1, P2)), ReplyA?,
                Pending, Pending1?) :-
    K? =?= B?, A? @< B?, P1? =?= A?, P2? =?= B? |
    peer_address(A?, PeerAddressA),
    match_reconnect(A?, B?, PeerAddressA?, ReplyA, Pending?, Pending1).

%% B signed the pair, B's key the smaller: admitted, as above.
admit_reconnect(A, B, signed(K, _, attest(P1, P2)), ReplyA?,
                Pending, Pending1?) :-
    K? =?= B?, B? @< A?, P1? =?= B?, P2? =?= A? |
    peer_address(A?, PeerAddressA),
    match_reconnect(A?, B?, PeerAddressA?, ReplyA, Pending?, Pending1).

%% unsigned, another signer, another term or the pair out of order: the
%% reply is bound to `rejected` and the request is not added to the pending
%% list.  That the signature verifies, and who made it, is signature/2's;
%% what the binding authorizes is this agent's logic.
admit_reconnect(_, _, _, rejected, Pending, Pending?) :-
    otherwise | true.

%% =============================================================================
%% admit_available: dispatch on what signature/2 reports for B's request
%% =============================================================================

exported procedure admit_available(Key?, Key?, Signature?, SignalReply,
                                   PendingList?, PendingList).

%% A signed the pair, A's key the smaller: admitted, under whatever program
%% A signed it.  Only then does the RV agent read B's address via
%% peer_address, and match_available/6 dispatches on what it reports.  The
%% guard grounds A and B.
admit_available(B, A, signed(K, _, attest(P1, P2)), ReplyB?,
                Pending, Pending1?) :-
    K? =?= A?, A? @< B?, P1? =?= A?, P2? =?= B? |
    peer_address(B?, PeerAddressB),
    match_available(B?, A?, PeerAddressB?, ReplyB, Pending?, Pending1).

%% A signed the pair, B's key the smaller: admitted, as above.
admit_available(B, A, signed(K, _, attest(P1, P2)), ReplyB?,
                Pending, Pending1?) :-
    K? =?= A?, B? @< A?, P1? =?= B?, P2? =?= A? |
    peer_address(B?, PeerAddressB),
    match_available(B?, A?, PeerAddressB?, ReplyB, Pending?, Pending1).

%% unsigned, another signer, another term or the pair out of order:
%% refused, as admit_reconnect/6 refuses.
admit_available(_, _, _, rejected, Pending, Pending?) :-
    otherwise | true.

%% =============================================================================
%% admit_register: dispatch on what signature/2 reports for a registration
%% =============================================================================

exported procedure admit_register(Key?, Signature?, SignalReply,
                                  PendingList?, PendingList).

%% The link is signed by the key the registration names, and names that key
%% as the inviter: admitted, under whatever program I signed it, and
%% register_serial/5 enters the serial as unused unless I has registered it
%% already.  The guard grounds I and the serial.
admit_register(I, signed(K, _, invitation(I1, _, _, Serial, _)), ReplyI?,
               Pending, Pending1?) :-
    K? =?= I?, I1? =?= I?, integer(Serial?) |
    register_serial(I?, Serial?, ReplyI, Pending?, Pending1).

%% unsigned, another signer, another term or another inviter: refused, and
%% nothing is registered.
admit_register(_, _, rejected, Pending, Pending?) :-
    otherwise | true.

%% =============================================================================
%% register_serial: enter I's serial as unused, once
%% =============================================================================

exported procedure register_serial(Key?, Integer?, SignalReply,
                                   PendingList?, PendingList).

%% I has registered this serial already, and it is unused: refused, and the
%% registration standing is left as it was.
register_serial(I, S, rejected, [invited(I2, S2, R2?)|Ps],
                [invited(I2?, S2?, R2)|Ps?]) :-
    I? =?= I2?, S? =?= S2? | true.

%% I has registered this serial already, and it is used: refused, so that a
%% link admits one first contact.
register_serial(I, S, rejected, [used(I2, S2, E2)|Ps],
                [used(I2?, S2?, E2?)|Ps?]) :-
    I? =?= I2?, S? =?= S2? | true.

%% Another entry: kept, and the search goes on.
register_serial(I, S, ReplyI?, [P|Ps], [P?|Ps1?]) :-
    otherwise |
    register_serial(I?, S?, ReplyI, Ps?, Ps1).

%% Not registered: entered as unused, I's reply awaiting the redemption.
register_serial(I, S, ReplyI?, [], [invited(I?, S?, ReplyI)]).

%% =============================================================================
%% admit_redeemer: dispatch on what signature/2 reports for a redemption
%% =============================================================================

%% admit_redeemer(Id, R, SR, T, ReplyR, Pending, Pending1): the redemption
%% by R at time T, signature/2 reporting SR for the signed term it carries
%% (GNA Section 6, "Invitation links": "Signed the term redeem(R, Link)
%% signed with its own key ... the redemption is signed by R").
exported procedure admit_redeemer(Key?, Key?, Signature?, Integer?,
                                  SignalReply, PendingList?, PendingList).

%% R signed redeem(R, Link): admitted, under whatever program R signed it,
%% and the link it carries is taken apart in turn, admit_redeem/7
%% dispatching on what signature/2 reports for it.  The guard grounds R.
admit_redeemer(Id, R, signed(K, _, redeem(R1, Link)), T, ReplyR?, Pending,
               Pending1?) :-
    K? =?= R?, R1? =?= R?, integer(T?) |
    signature(Link?, S),
    admit_redeem(Id?, R?, S?, T?, ReplyR, Pending?, Pending1).

%% unsigned, signed by another than the recipient it names, another term:
%% refused, and nothing is changed.
admit_redeemer(_, _, _, _, rejected, Pending, Pending?) :-
    otherwise | true.

%% =============================================================================
%% admit_redeem: dispatch on what signature/2 reports for the redeemed link,
%% and on the clock
%% =============================================================================

%% admit_redeem(Id, R, S, T, ReplyR, Pending, Pending1): the redemption by R
%% of the link signature/2 reports as S, at the agent of key Id, at time T.
exported procedure admit_redeem(Key?, Key?, Signature?, Integer?, SignalReply,
                                PendingList?, PendingList).

%% The link is signed by the inviter it names, names this agent, its Rv
%% being the agent's own key Id, and the clock T is before its expiry:
%% admitted, under whatever program the inviter signed it.  The RV agent
%% then reads the address at which the layer observes each of the two, and
%% redeem_addresses/8 dispatches on what it reports.
admit_redeem(Id, R, signed(K, _, invitation(I, Rv, _, Serial, Expiry)), T,
             ReplyR?, Pending, Pending1?) :-
    K? =?= I?, Rv? =?= Id?, integer(Serial?), integer(Expiry?),
    T? < Expiry? |
    peer_address(I?, PeerAddressI),
    peer_address(R?, PeerAddressR),
    redeem_addresses(I?, Serial?, Expiry?, PeerAddressI?, PeerAddressR?,
                     ReplyR, Pending?, Pending1).

%% unsigned, a link signed by another than the inviter it names, a link
%% naming another server, another term, or expired: refused, and nothing is
%% changed.
admit_redeem(_, _, _, _, rejected, Pending, Pending?) :-
    otherwise | true.

%% =============================================================================
%% redeem_addresses: dispatch on what peer_address/2 reports for the two
%% =============================================================================

%% redeem_addresses(I, Serial, Expiry, PeerAddressI, PeerAddressR, ReplyR,
%% Pending, Pending1): Expiry the link's, kept with the serial once used.
exported procedure redeem_addresses(Key?, Integer?, Integer?, PeerAddress?,
                                    PeerAddress?, SignalReply, PendingList?,
                                    PendingList).

%% The layer observes both: look for the serial, registered for the inviter
%% and unused.
redeem_addresses(I, S, E, address(AddrI), address(AddrR), ReplyR?,
                 Pending, Pending1?) :-
    find_invited(I?, S?, E?, AddrI?, AddrR?, ReplyR, Pending?, Pending1).

%% The layer observes no address for one of the two: there is none to relay,
%% so the redemption is refused and the serial is left as it was.
redeem_addresses(_, _, _, _, _, rejected, Pending, Pending?) :-
    otherwise | true.

%% =============================================================================
%% find_invited: the redemption arrived, search pending for the serial
%% registered for the inviter and unused
%% =============================================================================

%% find_invited(I, Serial, Expiry, AddrI, AddrR, ReplyR, Pending, Pending1).
exported procedure find_invited(Key?, Integer?, Integer?, String?, String?,
                                SignalReply, PendingList?, PendingList).

%% Registered and unused: marked used until the link's expiry E, and each is
%% told the address the RV agent observes for the other --- the inviter the
%% recipient's, the recipient the inviter's.
find_invited(I, S, E, AddrI, AddrR, ReplyR?, [invited(I2, S2, ReplyI?)|Ps],
             [used(I2?, S2?, E?)|Ps?]) :-
    I? =?= I2?, S? =?= S2? |
    complete_rendezvous(AddrI?, AddrR?, ReplyI, ReplyR).

%% Another entry, a used serial among them: kept, and the search goes on.
find_invited(I, S, E, AddrI, AddrR, ReplyR?, [P|Ps], [P?|Ps1?]) :-
    otherwise |
    find_invited(I?, S?, E?, AddrI?, AddrR?, ReplyR, Ps?, Ps1).

%% Not registered for the inviter, or used: refused.
find_invited(_, _, _, _, _, rejected, [], []).

%% =============================================================================
%% match_reconnect: dispatch on what peer_address/2 reports for A
%% =============================================================================

exported procedure match_reconnect(Key?, Key?, PeerAddress?, SignalReply,
                                   PendingList?, PendingList).

%% The layer observes A at AddrA: look for a waiting B.
match_reconnect(A, B, address(AddrA), ReplyA?, Pending, Pending1?) :-
    find_ready(A?, B?, AddrA?, ReplyA, Pending?, Pending1).

%% The layer observes no address for A: there is none to relay, so the
%% request is refused and not added to the pending list.
match_reconnect(_, _, none, rejected, Pending, Pending?).

%% =============================================================================
%% match_available: dispatch on what peer_address/2 reports for B
%% =============================================================================

exported procedure match_available(Key?, Key?, PeerAddress?, SignalReply,
                                   PendingList?, PendingList).

%% The layer observes B at AddrB: look for a waiting A.
match_available(B, A, address(AddrB), ReplyB?, Pending, Pending1?) :-
    find_needs(A?, B?, AddrB?, ReplyB, Pending?, Pending1).

%% The layer observes no address for B: refused, and not stored.
match_available(_, _, none, rejected, Pending, Pending?).

%% =============================================================================
%% find_ready: A arrived, search pending for a matching
%% ready(B, A, AddrB, ReplyB)
%% =============================================================================

exported procedure find_ready(Key?, Key?, String?, SignalReply,
                              PendingList?, PendingList).

%% Match found: B was waiting — assign replies
find_ready(A, B, AddrA, ReplyA?, [ready(B2, A2, AddrB, ReplyB?)|Ps], Ps?) :-
    A? =?= A2?, B? =?= B2? |
    complete_rendezvous(AddrA?, AddrB?, ReplyA, ReplyB).

%% No match at this entry, keep searching
find_ready(A, B, AddrA, ReplyA?, [P|Ps], [P?|Ps1?]) :-
    otherwise |
    find_ready(A?, B?, AddrA?, ReplyA, Ps?, Ps1).

%% End of list, no match: store A's request in pending
find_ready(A, B, AddrA, ReplyA?, [], [needs(A?, B?, AddrA?, ReplyA)]).

%% =============================================================================
%% find_needs: B arrived, search pending for a matching
%% needs(A, B, AddrA, ReplyA)
%% =============================================================================

exported procedure find_needs(Key?, Key?, String?, SignalReply,
                              PendingList?, PendingList).

%% Match found: A was waiting — assign replies
find_needs(A, B, AddrB, ReplyB?, [needs(A2, B2, AddrA, ReplyA?)|Ps], Ps?) :-
    A? =?= A2?, B? =?= B2? |
    complete_rendezvous(AddrA?, AddrB?, ReplyA, ReplyB).

%% No match at this entry, keep searching
find_needs(A, B, AddrB, ReplyB?, [P|Ps], [P?|Ps1?]) :-
    otherwise |
    find_needs(A?, B?, AddrB?, ReplyB, Ps?, Ps1).

%% End of list, no match: store B's availability in pending
find_needs(A, B, AddrB, ReplyB?, [], [ready(B?, A?, AddrB?, ReplyB)]).

%% =============================================================================
%% complete_rendezvous: Both sides matched — relay to each the address
%% observed for the other
%% =============================================================================

%% complete_rendezvous(AddrA, AddrB, ReplyA, ReplyB): A is told to punch to
%% B's address and B to A's.
procedure complete_rendezvous(String?, String?, SignalReply, SignalReply).
complete_rendezvous(AddrA, AddrB, punch(AddrB?), punch(AddrA?)).
